      "text": "A rainbow is a visually stunning natural phenomenon that occurs when sunlight interacts with water droplets in the atmosphere. The formation of a rainbow involves several key processes: refraction, reflection, dispersion, and viewing angle. Each of these processes plays a crucial role in creating the vibrant array of colors we observe in a rainbow.\n\nRefraction is the first step in the formation of a rainbow. When sunlight enters a water droplet, it is refracted, or bent, due to the change in the density of air and water. This bending of light is caused by the difference in the refractive index between air and water. As the light enters the droplet, it slows down and bends due to the change in medium. The refractive index of water is higher than that of air, causing the light to bend towards the normal (the line perpendicular to the surface). This bending of light is known as refraction.\n\nThe refracted light then travels through the water droplet, and as it exits, it undergoes another refraction. This second refraction causes the light to disperse into its component colors. The different colors of light have different wavelengths, and as they exit the droplet, they are refracted again, causing them to separate further. This process is known as dispersion",
